st-xclip(1) -- xclip that sanitizes a clipboard write
=====================================================

<!--
# Copyright (C) 2026 - 2026 ENCRYPTED SUPPORT LLC <adrelanos@whonix.org>
# See the file COPYING for copying conditions.
-->

## SYNOPSIS

`st-xclip` [<XCLIP-OPTION>...]

## DESCRIPTION

st-xclip is a drop-in replacement for `xclip`(1) (X11) that filters standard
input through `sclip`(1) on a clipboard WRITE. Every argument is forwarded
verbatim to `xclip`, so native options such as `-selection clipboard` work
unchanged.

xclip is one command for both directions, so st-xclip uses a simple, honest rule
rather than parsing xclip's option grammar:

  * a READ (`-o` / `-out`), or `-help` / `-version`, is passed through unchanged;
  * a piped standard input that is not a read is sanitized through `sclip`;
  * anything else (standard input is a terminal: a file-operand write, or an
    interactive invocation) is passed through unchanged.

So it sanitizes the common `echo ... | st-xclip -selection clipboard` form. For a
file-operand write (`xclip file`) use the raw pipeline instead:

    sclip < file | xclip -selection clipboard

By default the strip is strictest (printable ASCII only). Set the environment
variable `SCLIP_MODE=unicode` to keep printable non-ASCII instead.

## ENVIRONMENT

  * `SCLIP_MODE`:
    When set to `unicode`, keep printable non-ASCII (passes `--unicode` to
    `sclip`). Any other value (or unset) is the default ASCII-only strip.

## EXIT STATUS

The exit status of `xclip`, or of the `sclip` | `xclip` pipeline on a sanitized
write; 127 if `xclip` is not installed.

## SEE ALSO

`st-wl-paste`(1), `st-wl-copy`(1), `sclip`(1), `xclip`(1)
